# The Deck (Serra variant) — A STUDY LIST, AND THE ONLY PLAYABLE DECK IN
#   THE TREE THAT HOLDS A FEEDER AND A CREATURE AT ONCE. Two Serra Angels
#   for two Mishra's Factories, which is the whole difference from
#   `the_deck_playable.deck` — itself The Deck with one card changed
#   (Nevinyrral's Disk for Chaos Orb, and its own header carries that
#   provenance).
#
# READ THIS BEFORE PICKING IT UP: IT IS NOT A BETTER THE DECK, AND IT WAS
#   NEVER SHOWN TO BE ONE. It shipped on 2026-09-10 as the Angel row's own
#   list and was settled on 2026-09-11 with the census below. Anyone
#   wanting The Deck to play should take `the_deck_playable.deck`; anyone
#   wanting the Angel should take
#   `../community/the_deck_weissman_1994_95_winter.deck`, which is the
#   shape that actually closes (below). This file's job is measurement.
#
# WHAT IT IS FOR. The Abyss eats a creature at EVERY player's upkeep, its
#   controller's included, so a list holding three copies of it AND two
#   five-mana bodies is the only place in `decks/` where two readings of
#   the pilot can fire at all: the appetite already in play
#   (`AiPlayer._fed_on_arrival`, inside `checks_before_casting`) and the
#   refused field `AiProfile.holds_the_closer`. Every other playable deck
#   in the tree holds the feeder with no creature (`the_deck_playable`) or
#   creatures with no feeder (everything else) — the two lists that hold
#   The Abyss in a SIDEBOARD never see it in free play, and the seven
#   other decks that hold it maindeck name a card the pool does not
#   implement and cannot be dealt at all. Take this file away and both
#   readings become unmeasurable. That, and nothing about its win rate, is
#   why it stays; DeckLab/README.md names it as the live pair for both
#   sweeps.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, measured 2026-09-11 against the five starters, 150 games
#   a matchup, seed 4242, Wizard on both seats, `--mulligan on`, the
#   appetite reading ON:
#
#     list                        win rate    win turn   by library-out
#     the_deck_playable.deck    50.8% (381-369)   50.3    161 of 381 (42%)
#     this list                 49.3% (370-380)   52.9    244 of 370 (66%)
#
#   Game for game on the same seeds, 173 of the 750 changed hands — 81 to
#   a win for the Angels against 92 away, where a fair toss over 173 sits
#   at 86.5 +-6.6. So the win rate is a WASH and the SPEED is not: the
#   Angel list wins two and a half turns LATER and two thirds of its wins
#   are the opponent drawing from an empty library, against two fifths for
#   the list it varies. The Angel closes when it lands — the wins by
#   DAMAGE come at turn 38.4 here against 46.6 there — but it lands
#   seldom: 533 Angels cast in 750 games, 210 of them eaten at our own
#   upkeep by our own enchantment even with the appetite reading on. The
#   reading refuses a cast into a feeder ALREADY ON THE TABLE; it cannot
#   refuse a feeder drawn or replayed afterwards, and `_in_danger` lifts
#   it when the body has to block. Two Factories out is two lands out, and
#   what replaces them is a card the deck's own table will not let it
#   play.
#
# AND THE SHAPE THE ANGEL WANTS IS ALREADY IN THE TREE. On the same
#   census `../community/the_deck_weissman_1994_95_winter.deck` — four
#   Serra Angels, two Moats, no Abyss, no Factory — measures 48.8%
#   (366-384) and wins at turn 29.3, TWENTY-ONE TURNS SOONER than the
#   playable list, with 355 of its 366 wins by damage and 11 by
#   library-out. That is the Angel item's own claim ("ten to twenty turns
#   sooner, and nothing about the loss": its losses come at 19.8 on the
#   mean against 19.3) delivered by a list that already ships. Half
#   conversions are what fail: this list keeps the Abyss and gets no
#   speed, and the same shell with the three feeders out for two Moats and
#   a Factory measures 36.4% and the one-Abyss middle 40.7%.
#
# WHAT CHANGED FROM THE BASE LIST, exactly: -2 Mishra's Factory, +2 Serra
#   Angel. The base list holds THREE Factories, not four (the plan's own
#   note says four; the file says three, and the file is what plays), so
#   one Factory stays: the deck still wants a land that blocks a Savannah
#   Lions on a turn its mana is spoken for.
#
#   Nothing else moves. The mana is untouched — the Angel's {3}{W}{W} is
#   paid by four City of Brass, three Tundra, one Plains and the Moxen
#   the list already runs for Swords to Plowshares and Disenchant, which
#   want the same white and want it earlier.
#
# NOT A COMMUNITY LIST. It ships under "Playable variants" for the same
#   reason `the_deck_playable.deck` does: the community folders are a
#   provenance ledger of decks somebody really published, and this one is
#   ours.
#
# archetype: The Deck — Old School 93/94, made playable, with a finisher
# designer: Randy Buehler (base list); the two changes are this project's
# year: August 2015 (base list), 2026-09-10 (the Angels)
# source: decks/variants/the_deck_playable.deck, which carries the full
#   provenance; the Angel's shape is Weissman's Winter 1994-95 list
# group: Playable variants
name: The Deck (Serra variant)
